2. Codebook sources
   The episode codes and their operational definitions are cited in
   codebook.v2.json, in its `source` field and in each code's `origin`:
   Schoenfeld (1985) for the categories; Li et al. (2025, EMNLP 2025,
   doi:10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.922, Appendix E; CC BY 4.0, ©2025
   Association for Computational Linguistics; see §4) for the operational
   definitions; Rott, Specht & Knipping (2021, ZDM 53(4):737-752) for two
   inductive additions. The definitions of the seven codes whose origin is
   Li et al. are adapted from its Appendix E, with what was adapted recorded
   per code; organization and digression are adapted from Rott et al. The example
   sentences in codebook.v2.json are quoted, wholly or partly, from that
   appendix. codebook.v2.json itself still cites the arXiv preprint,
   arXiv:2509.14662.

   Zhou, Vanacore, Thompson, St John, Kizilcec. Tutor Move Taxonomy: A
   Theory-Aligned Framework for Analyzing Instructional Moves in Tutoring.
   arXiv:2603.05778, 2026. doi:10.48550/arXiv.2603.05778. CC BY 4.0
   (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Used by Layer 2, an
   independent layer cited in codebook.tutor-moves.json, in its `source`
   field and in each code's `origin`. Three code names are taken verbatim
   from its Table 1: PROMPTING_SELF_EXPLANATION, PROMPTING_SELF_CORRECTION
   and GIVING_ANSWER. No definition text is reproduced; each code is stated
   in this project's own words. Cohen's kappa as the authors report it, for
   the full scheme: .65 in the first round and .78 in the second round,
   after clarifications and minor alterations.

